# Problem Statement

Create a harness for my repo in a generic manner that does these below things
- get the context from a repo, knowledge documents
- read a spec document for the implementation of a new feature
- read the data/design source (source schema, sample-data profile, dashboard mock)
- answer clarifying questions for the spec/design from human in the loop
- create a technical design
- review and approve the technical design from human in the loop
- implement the features - code creation
- publish PR, suggest test coverage, and review from human in the loop 
- retrospect, next improvement

---

# Design: A GitHub Copilot Harness for a Data Engineering / Data Science Repo

## What this is

A **hybrid of two cooperating planes**. GitHub Copilot — running as a custom agent
inside Visual Studio Code — is the **execution plane** that does the coding.
Microsoft Agent Framework — a Python or .NET process running outside the editor —
is the **governance plane** that observes, evaluates, and guards that work.
GitHub Copilot cannot be driven *as* an application programming interface, and
Microsoft Agent Framework cannot type in your editor, so each owns the half it is
good at. They meet at shared, file-based artifacts in the repository.

| Plane | Runs where | Owns |
|---|---|---|
| **Execution plane** — GitHub Copilot | Custom agent inside Visual Studio Code | Reads context, asks clarifying questions, writes the technical design, implements the code, opens the pull request. |
| **Governance plane** — Microsoft Agent Framework | Python or .NET process outside the editor | OpenTelemetry tracing, automated evaluation of the design and tests, and middleware that redacts secrets and personally identifiable information. |

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph EX[Execution plane -- GitHub Copilot in Visual Studio Code]
        AG[data-feature.agent.md] <-->|AskUserQuestion| HU[Human]
    end
    subgraph GOV[Governance plane -- Microsoft Agent Framework]
        MW[Middleware: secret / PII redaction]
        OT[OpenTelemetry tracing]
        EV[Evaluation: design + tests]
        CP[Context providers / retrieval-augmented generation]
    end
    FILES[[Shared files: copilot-instructions.md, contracts/, docs/]]
    AG --- FILES
    CP --> FILES
    GOV -.observes.- EX
```

## The only files you need

The **entire harness folder structure lives inside the implementation repository
itself** (same repo, in-place) — there is no separate harness repo. GitHub Copilot
and every change it makes operate on that same working tree, and
`.github/copilot-instructions.md` holds the **reference/map to that repo**.

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `.github/copilot-instructions.md` | Always-on **reference to the repository itself**: the folder map, where implementation changes go, build / test commands, coding conventions, and where the data contracts live. GitHub Copilot reads this automatically on every request. |
| `contracts/` | The source of truth, in YAML: table and column **schemas**, **metric definitions**, and freshness / ownership service-level agreements. |
| `.github/agents/data-feature.agent.md` | One GitHub Copilot custom agent that runs all nine steps and stops at the human approval gates. |
| `docs/specs/<feature>.md` | Input: the feature or metric request you hand to the agent. |
| `docs/design/<feature>.md` | Output of step 5: the technical design (written from a short template). |
| `docs/progress/<feature>.md` | A plain Markdown checklist the agent keeps up to date so any session can resume where it left off. |

That is the execution-plane harness. Everything else — multiple agents, a
validator runner, a state-machine file — is optional. The Microsoft Agent
Framework governance plane (described below) is the second half of the hybrid.

## How the nine steps run

One agent, three human approval gates. The agent uses the `AskUserQuestion` tool
at each gate so choices are explicit named options, never a free-text "Proceed?".

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[1. Read context<br/>copilot-instructions.md + contracts/] --> B[2. Read spec<br/>docs/specs/feature.md]
    B --> C[3. Read schema + profile sample data]
    C --> D{4. Clarifying questions<br/>HUMAN APPROVES}
    D --> E[5. Write technical design<br/>docs/design/feature.md]
    E --> F{6. Review design<br/>HUMAN APPROVES}
    F -->|changes| E
    F -->|approved| G[7. Implement<br/>Synapse / Databricks / Fabric notebooks + pipelines]
    G --> H{8. Open pull request + tests<br/>HUMAN APPROVES}
    H -->|changes| G
    H -->|approved| I[9. Retrospective<br/>note improvements, pick next]
    I -.next feature.-> A
```

| Step | What the agent does | Human gate |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Get context | Reads `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, the `contracts/` folder, and any knowledge documents in the repository. | — |
| 2. Read spec | Reads `docs/specs/<feature>.md`. | — |
| 3. Read data / design source | Reads the source and target **table schemas** and runs a quick **data profile** (row counts, null percentage, value distributions, cardinality). Uses a dashboard mock if one exists. | — |
| 4. Clarifying questions | Asks about **grain**, **slowly changing dimension type**, **backfill window**, and **personally identifiable information** handling. | ✅ human answers |
| 5. Technical design | Writes `docs/design/<feature>.md`: target tables, transformation logic, and the data-quality tests to add. | — |
| 6. Approve design | — | ✅ human approves or requests changes |
| 7. Implement | Writes the code: **Microsoft Fabric**, **Azure Synapse Analytics**, or **Azure Databricks** notebooks and pipelines, and the supporting Spark / SQL and orchestration assets. | — |
| 8. Pull request + tests | Opens a **pull request** and adds tests — **dbt tests** or **Great Expectations** checks, a schema-contract check, and an **idempotent backfill dry run**. | ✅ human reviews before merge |
| 9. Retrospective | Records what to improve (runtime, cost / bytes scanned, data freshness) and proposes the next feature. | — |

## Human-in-the-loop, kept simple

Only three gates matter: **clarifying questions (step 4)**, **design approval
(step 6)**, and **pull-request review (step 8)**. At each gate the agent uses the
`AskUserQuestion` tool with a few named options and waits. Between gates it works
on its own and keeps `docs/progress/<feature>.md` current.

## The governance plane: where Microsoft Agent Framework fits

Think of Microsoft Agent Framework as the **supervisor that sits outside your
editor**. GitHub Copilot writes the code; this plane watches, checks, and controls
the model calls that happen *away* from the editor.

- **What it is** — a small Python program you run separately (`pip install
  agent-framework`). It is not a Visual Studio Code extension.
- **What it touches** — only the *non-editor* model calls: analyzing the spec,
  scoring the design, running the retrospective. It never types in your editor.
- **Why it matters** — it is where you put the rules you cannot enforce from
  inside GitHub Copilot: redaction, tracing, evaluation gates, and shared tools.

Each capability below is explained in plain language, then shown with the exact
Microsoft Agent Framework code that implements it.

### 1. Middleware — redact secrets / PII and log every model call

- **The idea** — middleware is code that runs *around* every model call, like a
  security checkpoint at an airport gate: nothing reaches the model without
  passing through it.
- **Two kinds you use here:**
  - **Agent middleware** — runs once per request, *before* the model. Use it to
    **block** a request outright (for example, one that leaks a password).
  - **Chat middleware** — runs on *every* individual model call. Use it to
    **log** or **scrub** the messages that are sent.
- **In the code below:**
  - `block_secrets` checks the last message; if it finds a credential it sets a
    canned reply and returns *without* calling `call_next()`, so the model is
    never contacted.
  - `log_calls` prints before and after each model call.
  - Both are registered once via `middleware=[...]`, so they apply to every run.

```python
import re
from collections.abc import Awaitable, Callable

from agent_framework import (
    Agent, AgentContext, AgentResponse, ChatContext, Message,
    agent_middleware, chat_middleware,
)
from agent_framework.foundry import FoundryChatClient
from azure.identity.aio import AzureCliCredential

SECRET = re.compile(r"(?i)(password|secret|token|api[_-]?key)\s*[:=]\s*\S+")

@agent_middleware
async def block_secrets(context: AgentContext, call_next: Callable[[], Awaitable[None]]) -> None:
    last = context.messages[-1] if context.messages else None
    if last and last.text and SECRET.search(last.text):
        # Set the result and skip call_next() -> the model is never called.
        context.result = AgentResponse(
            messages=[Message("assistant", ["Blocked: request contained a secret."])]
        )
        return
    await call_next()

@chat_middleware
async def log_calls(context: ChatContext, call_next: Callable[[], Awaitable[None]]) -> None:
    print(f"[governance] sending {len(context.messages)} messages to the model")
    await call_next()
    print("[governance] model responded")

async def main() -> None:
    async with AzureCliCredential() as credential:
        async with Agent(
            client=FoundryChatClient(credential=credential),
            name="SpecReviewer",
            instructions="Analyze the feature spec and list the gaps.",
            middleware=[block_secrets, log_calls],   # applies to every run
        ) as agent:
            print((await agent.run("Review docs/specs/feature.md")).text)
```

### 2. OpenTelemetry — trace the whole lifecycle

- **The idea** — OpenTelemetry is the industry-standard way to record *what
  happened and how long it took*. Each recorded step is called a **span**.
- **What you get for free** — Microsoft Agent Framework already emits spans for
  every agent run and tool call, following the generative-AI naming conventions,
  so they appear in any backend (Azure Monitor, Jaeger) with no extra code.
- **What the code adds** — a one-time exporter setup (where the spans are sent),
  plus a custom span around each model call so you can line governance activity up
  against GitHub Copilot's own run logs.
- **Why it matters** — when a run misbehaves you can see the whole lifecycle in
  one trace instead of guessing from scattered logs.

```python
from opentelemetry import trace
from opentelemetry.sdk.trace import TracerProvider
from opentelemetry.sdk.trace.export import BatchSpanProcessor
from opentelemetry.exporter.otlp.proto.grpc.trace_exporter import OTLPSpanExporter
from agent_framework import ChatContext, chat_middleware

trace.set_tracer_provider(TracerProvider())
trace.get_tracer_provider().add_span_processor(BatchSpanProcessor(OTLPSpanExporter()))
tracer = trace.get_tracer("data-harness.governance")

@chat_middleware
async def trace_calls(context: ChatContext, call_next) -> None:
    with tracer.start_as_current_span("gen_ai.model.call") as span:
        span.set_attribute("gen_ai.request.message_count", len(context.messages))
        await call_next()
```

### 3. Context providers (retrieval-augmented generation) — refresh the instructions file

- **The problem** — `.github/copilot-instructions.md` goes stale the moment a
  table schema or column changes, and stale context makes GitHub Copilot write
  wrong code.
- **The fix (retrieval-augmented generation)** — instead of editing that file by
  hand, the governance plane *retrieves* the current facts and *regenerates* the
  file automatically:
  1. **Retrieve** — a vector search returns the most relevant, up-to-date schema
     and lineage facts from the data catalog and `contracts/` (`catalog_snippets`).
  2. **Generate** — a small agent turns those facts into a concise instructions
     file.
  3. **Write** — the result overwrites `.github/copilot-instructions.md`.
- **When to run it** — on a schedule, or whenever a data contract changes, so
  GitHub Copilot always reads the latest ground truth.
- **In-run memory (optional)** — to inject context *during* an agent run instead
  of into a file, attach a provider with the `context_providers=[...]` argument.

```python
from pathlib import Path
from agent_framework import Agent
from agent_framework.foundry import FoundryChatClient
from azure.identity.aio import AzureCliCredential

async def refresh_copilot_instructions(catalog_snippets: list[str]) -> None:
    """catalog_snippets = top-k results from your vector search over catalog + contracts/."""
    async with AzureCliCredential() as credential:
        async with Agent(
            client=FoundryChatClient(credential=credential),
            name="ContextWriter",
            instructions=(
                "Write a concise .github/copilot-instructions.md: repo map, where "
                "changes go, build / test commands, and data-contract locations."
            ),
        ) as agent:
            facts = "\n\n".join(catalog_snippets)
            md = await agent.run(f"Source facts:\n{facts}")
            Path(".github/copilot-instructions.md").write_text(md.text, encoding="utf-8")
```

### 4. Evaluation — score the design and tests, then gate the pull request

- **The idea** — an "**LLM-as-judge**": one agent grades the work of another
  against a rubric and returns a machine-readable score you can act on.
- **What it checks:**
  - the technical design against the **data contracts** — did it cover every
    required table, column, and metric? — and
  - the **data-quality tests** for adequate coverage. These are assertions about
    the data itself (no nulls, values in range, keys unique, row counts within
    tolerance) written with **Great Expectations** — an open-source Python library
    for data-quality testing, the "unit tests" of a data pipeline — or with
    **Microsoft Fabric / Azure Synapse** built-in data-quality rules.
- **What it returns** — strict JSON: `{"pass": bool, "missing": [...],
  "coverage": 0.0-1.0}`, so no human has to read it to decide.
- **How you use it (the gate)** — if `pass` is `false`, the pipeline stops before
  the pull request opens (`raise SystemExit`). This turns a subjective review into
  an automatic quality gate.

```python
import json
from agent_framework import Agent
from agent_framework.foundry import FoundryChatClient
from azure.identity.aio import AzureCliCredential

async def evaluate_design(design_md: str, contracts_yaml: str) -> dict:
    async with AzureCliCredential() as credential:
        async with Agent(
            client=FoundryChatClient(credential=credential),
            name="DesignJudge",
            instructions=(
                "Score the design against the data contracts. Return only JSON: "
                '{"pass": bool, "missing": [str], "coverage": 0.0-1.0}.'
            ),
        ) as agent:
            result = await agent.run(f"DESIGN:\n{design_md}\n\nCONTRACTS:\n{contracts_yaml}")
            return json.loads(result.text)

verdict = await evaluate_design(design_md, contracts_yaml)
if not verdict["pass"]:
    raise SystemExit(f"Design gate failed — missing: {verdict['missing']}")
```

### 5. Workflows with checkpointing — optional unattended driver

- **When to use it** — only if you want the nine steps to run **unattended** (for
  example, kicked off by a continuous-integration pipeline) instead of a person
  driving GitHub Copilot in the editor. For interactive work you do **not** need
  this.
- **The building blocks:**
  - **Step / executor** — one unit of work (for example, "create design").
  - **Human gate** — `ctx.request_info()` pauses the run and waits for a person's
    answer, then continues. Same approval idea as `AskUserQuestion`, but for an
    unattended run.
  - **Checkpoint** — the run's state is saved at each step boundary, so a job that
    pauses for approval can **resume days later** exactly where it stopped.
- **Two ways to write it** — the sketch below uses the simpler **Functional
  Workflow API** (`@workflow` / `@step`). The **graph API** is the same idea with
  `WorkflowBuilder`, a `RequestInfoExecutor` for the gates, and superstep-boundary
  checkpoints. (Check the Workflows docs for exact signatures.)

```python
# Functional Workflow API (Python). Human gates use ctx.request_info();
# checkpointing lets an unattended run pause and resume. See the Workflows docs
# for exact signatures.
from agent_framework import workflow, step, WorkflowContext

@step
async def create_design(ctx: WorkflowContext, spec: str) -> str:
    ...  # call the design agent; return the technical-design markdown

@step
async def approve_design(ctx: WorkflowContext, design: str) -> str:
    decision = await ctx.request_info(prompt=f"Approve this design?\n{design}")  # HITL pause
    return design if decision == "approved" else await create_design(ctx, design)

@workflow(checkpointing=True)
async def feature_flow(ctx: WorkflowContext, spec: str) -> str:
    design = await create_design(ctx, spec)
    return await approve_design(ctx, design)
```

### 6. Model Context Protocol clients — one shared tool layer

- **What MCP is** — Model Context Protocol is a standard way for an agent to talk
  to an outside system (GitHub, a data warehouse) through a small "server" that
  exposes tools. Think of it as a **universal adapter** for tools.
- **Why it matters here** — the *same* GitHub MCP server that GitHub Copilot uses
  is also usable by your governance agents, so both planes share one tool layer
  instead of each re-implementing GitHub access.
- **How the code wires it up:**
  - `client.get_mcp_tool(...)` attaches a hosted MCP server by URL.
  - `headers` carries the authentication token (kept in an environment variable,
    never hard-coded).
  - `approval_mode="always_require"` forces a human to sign off before any risky
    write, such as opening a pull request or posting a comment.

```python
import os
from agent_framework import Agent
from agent_framework.openai import OpenAIChatClient

client = OpenAIChatClient()
github_mcp = client.get_mcp_tool(
    name="GitHub",
    url="https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/",
    headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {os.environ['GITHUB_PAT']}"},
    approval_mode="always_require",   # require sign-off before writes (PR, comments)
)

async with Agent(
    client=client,
    name="PRGovernor",
    instructions="Open and annotate the pull request for the approved slice.",
    tools=[github_mcp],
) as agent:
    print((await agent.run("Open a draft PR for feature/x and suggest test coverage")).text)
```

- **Data-stack tools** — for the Microsoft data stack, a **Microsoft Fabric data
  agent** tool is also available through the Foundry provider (preview) to query
  the lakehouse, alongside the GitHub, warehouse (Microsoft Fabric, Azure Synapse
  Analytics, Azure Databricks), and data-catalog (Microsoft Purview) servers
  shared across both planes.

